const articleHtml = "<p>The report says inconclusive. It is not a synonym for negative, male, female or failed. It is a statement that the evidence did not meet the laboratory's validated criteria for a classification. That can happen before the sample reaches the bench, inside the reaction, or at the reporting desk.</p>\n<p>It belongs to the wider guide on <a href=\"/bird-dna-testing-methods-accuracy/\">bird DNA testing methods and accuracy</a> and explains where an inconclusive call comes from, how it differs from a negative, and what to do next.</p>\n<h2>What an Inconclusive Result Actually Says</h2>\n<p>An inconclusive result says the laboratory cannot defend a call. Three situations produce it. The sample may not contain enough usable DNA. The reaction may produce a signal outside the validated calling window. Or a quality control may fail in a way that cannot be resolved.</p>\n<p>Those are different problems with different fixes. Ask which situation applies; a laboratory that cannot say is hiding behind the label.</p>\n<p>Inconclusive is also a decision rule, not a diagnosis. The rule should be written before samples arrive: what counts as sufficient DNA, which controls must pass, what Ct range or band pattern supports each call, and what happens when replicates disagree. A laboratory that never reports inconclusive is not necessarily better. It may be converting uncertainty into a confident answer.</p>\n<h2>Positive, Negative, Inconclusive: Three Different Claims</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>The claim being made</th>\n<th>What supports it</th>\n<th>What it leaves open</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td>Target absent from the reportable range</td>\n<td>Run valid; controls and adequacy check pass</td>\n<td>Whether the bird is truly free of the target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inconclusive</td>\n<td>No defensible classification</td>\n<td>Ambiguous signal, conflicting replicates, poor sample, or unresolved control</td>\n<td>Whether the bird is negative, male or clean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invalid</td>\n<td>No result exists</td>\n<td>Run or sample failure, processing error, broken chain</td>\n<td>Anything about the bird</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Laboratories use different words for the middle row: indeterminate, equivocal, repeat required, no call. Ask what the word means. A \"repeat\" can mean the extract will be run again, or that a new sample is required.</p>\n<p>A negative is a finding; an inconclusive is the absence of one. The gap matters most in sexing, where \"no W band\" is easy to report as male even when nothing amplified.</p>\n<h2>Pre-Analytical Causes: The Sample Sets the Ceiling</h2>\n<p>Most inconclusive results begin before the tube reaches the laboratory: sample quality sets the ceiling.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample type.</strong> A freshly plucked feather with a visible follicle contains dividing cells and template. A shed feather may contain none. Blood is rich in DNA but can carry inhibitors. A cloacal swab carries host cells, bacteria and polysaccharides (Turcu et al., 2023). Formalin-fixed tissue is crosslinked and degraded.</p>\n<p><strong>Storage and shipping.</strong> Heat, moisture and delay reduce intact template. A feather left in a car, a swab shipped without drying, or a blood tube thawed twice can arrive clean by appearance and useless by assay. The <a href=\"/sample-shipping/\">sample shipping guide</a> covers the practical side.</p>\n<p><strong>Collection contamination.</strong> Two birds sampled with the same gloves, feathers from different birds in one envelope, or an egg-membrane sample carrying maternal blood can produce a mixed template. A mixed sample may amplify, but the pattern does not belong to one bird.</p>\n<p><strong>Degradation.</strong> DNA breaks into fragments, and longer amplicons fail first. A sample can yield a decent concentration reading and still fail a 400-base-pair target.</p>\n<p>Quantity and quality are separate problems. An extract can have enough DNA and still be inhibited, or be clean and contain too few copies. The first needs cleanup; the second needs more sample.</p>\n<h2>Analytical Causes: Inhibition, Dropout, and Weak Signal</h2>\n<p>Inhibition is the most common analytical cause. Melanin, heme, polysaccharides and residues of guanidine, ethanol or EDTA can block the polymerase (Schrader et al., 2012). Partial inhibition appears as a late Ct, a low peak or a skewed ratio; severe inhibition appears as nothing. An internal amplification control is the only way to distinguish a negative from a suppressed reaction.</p>\n<p>Allele dropout is the failure of one target while another succeeds. In sexing, CHD-W disappears and a female reads as male. Causes include a primer mismatch at the W site, a longer W amplicon that fragments first, low copy number and PCR bias toward the shorter Z product.</p>\n<p>Non-specific amplification produces a product from an unintended site: an extra band, a smear or a late signal with the wrong melt temperature. In a sexing assay it can mimic CHD-W; in a pathogen assay it can create a false detection.</p>\n<p>Heteroduplexes form when related strands, such as CHD-Z and CHD-W or two CHD-Z variants, reanneal to the wrong partner after the final denaturation. The mismatched duplex migrates abnormally and can appear as an extra band. A laboratory that counts bands without size standards may call that band W; a validated pattern may call the sample inconclusive instead.</p>\n<p>Resolution is the last limit. If W and Z differ by only a few bases, a gel may show one band even when both are present. <a href=\"/how-capillary-electrophoresis-analysis-works-in-bird-sexing-and-parentage-testing/\">Capillary electrophoresis</a> or sequencing can resolve the pattern; a repeat on the same gel cannot.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cause</th>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Typical signal</th>\n<th>Next step</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Insufficient DNA</td>\n<td>Pre-analytical</td>\n<td>Late or absent amplification, low yield</td>\n<td>New sample or re-extract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Degradation</td>\n<td>Pre-analytical</td>\n<td>Longer amplicons fail first</td>\n<td>New sample</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inhibition</td>\n<td>Analytical</td>\n<td>Late Ct, low peak, failed internal control</td>\n<td>Dilute, purify or re-extract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CHD-W dropout</td>\n<td>Analytical</td>\n<td>Strong Z, absent W</td>\n<td>Alternative primer or new extract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-specific amplification</td>\n<td>Analytical</td>\n<td>Extra band, wrong melt peak</td>\n<td>Repeat at higher stringency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heteroduplex formation</td>\n<td>Analytical</td>\n<td>Slow extra band or doublet</td>\n<td>Reanneal, use capillary electrophoresis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fragment co-migration</td>\n<td>Analytical</td>\n<td>One band at Z size</td>\n<td>Higher-resolution platform</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Quality Control Failures and Batch Invalidation</h2>\n<p>Each control answers a narrow question. The no-template control tests reagents and workspace for amplifiable DNA. The extraction blank tests the extraction workflow. The positive control tests whether the assay can amplify a known target. The internal control tests whether a given extract can support amplification.</p>\n<p>A failed control can make a sample inconclusive even when its own lane looks convincing. A band in the no-template control means the run is contaminated, so every positive in it is suspect. Amplicon carryover and contaminated reagents produce the same signal, which is why blanks accompany every batch. A single band from the positive female control means W failed that day, so every male call is suspect. A failed internal control means the extract was inadequate even if the target produced signal.</p>\n<p>The response is batch-level, not sample-level. A laboratory should not keep convenient lanes and discard inconvenient ones. It should invalidate the run, investigate and repeat.</p>\n<h2>Why No Amplification Is Not a Male Result</h2>\n<p>This is the most common confusion in avian sexing. A male is ZZ and a female is ZW. In a <a href=\"/the-complete-guide-to-bird-dna-sexing/\">CHD assay</a>, a male normally shows one Z product and a female shows Z plus W. So \"no W\" looks like male.</p>\n<p>The inference only holds when three conditions are met. CHD-Z amplified in that sample. The sample passed its adequacy check. The assay has been validated for the species. If neither Z nor W amplifies, the tube holds no interpretable data. The correct report is inconclusive, not male.</p>\n<p>The same logic applies to pathogen testing. A negative requires a valid run and a passing internal control. If the internal control fails, a blank target lane is inconclusive. A negative means the target was below the limit of detection, not that the bird is free of the organism.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/avian-molecular-biology-guide-the-foundation-of-bird-dna-testing/\">avian molecular biology guide</a> explains the reaction-level difference between a true negative and a failed amplification. The distinction decides whether a breeder culls, pairs or waits.</p>\n<h2>What a Laboratory Should Do After an Inconclusive</h2>\n<p>A structured review beats an immediate repeat.</p>\n<p>First, check the raw data: curves, melt peaks, band sizes, control lanes and sample identity. Do not repeat until the cause is understood.</p>\n<p>Second, assess the sample. Quantify DNA, inspect the extraction record and look for inhibitors. If the extract is dilute or dirty, re-extract from the original material when enough remains.</p>\n<p>Third, repeat the assay on the new extract. If the same pattern appears, switch to an alternative primer set, capillary electrophoresis or sequencing. A different method gives independent evidence; the same method on the same extract often reproduces the same artifact.</p>\n<p>Fourth, report what happened. If the evidence still does not support a call, the inconclusive category stays. A laboratory should never convert an inconclusive result into a definitive answer to clear a queue.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Re-extract</th>\n<th>New sample</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Adequate material remains, extract looks dirty or dilute</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Only if re-extract fails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No follicle tissue, shed feather, degraded material</td>\n<td>Unlikely to help</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primer mismatch suspected</td>\n<td>Try alternative primer first</td>\n<td>Yes if the bird can be resampled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control failure with a valid sample</td>\n<td>Repeat the run</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mixed or mislabeled sample</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Yes, with verified identity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>What Owners and Breeders Should Do Next</h2>\n<p>Ask four questions. Which category is this: negative, inconclusive or invalid? Which controls failed, if any? What is the stated reason for the call? What sample would the laboratory prefer next?</p>\n<p>Then resample properly. Pluck fresh feathers with visible follicle tissue, one bird per envelope, gloves between birds, label before sampling, dry and ship promptly. If the bird is molting, wait for a fresh feather rather than sending a shed one.</p>\n<p>Do not demand a forced answer. A laboratory that offers to \"make it male\" or \"make it female\" for an extra fee is offering a guess, not a test. If two independent samples give the same inconclusive pattern, the honest answer may stay inconclusive; a different marker or <a href=\"/contact/\">a different laboratory</a> is the next step.</p>\n<p>For a breeding decision, treat inconclusive as a pause. Do not pair, sell or cull on a result that does not exist. Record the sample type, shipping date and laboratory response. The <a href=\"/how-much-does-it-cost-to-dna-test-a-bird/\">cost guide</a> explains why a repeat or a second method may add expense without adding certainty.</p>\n<h2>Species and Sample Types With Higher Inconclusive Rates</h2>\n<p><strong>Ratites.</strong> Standard CHD primers were developed for non-ratite birds. Emu, ostrich and kiwi have a different sex-chromosome arrangement, so a CHD assay may fail or mislead. Emu sexing needs a validated method for that group; see <a href=\"/how-to-tell-emu-gender/\">how to tell emu gender</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Species with similar fragment sizes.</strong> When CHD-Z and CHD-W differ by only a few bases, agarose gels may not resolve them. Capillary electrophoresis is the usual fix. <a href=\"/bird-species-dna-testing/\">Bird species DNA testing</a> covers compatibility more broadly.</p>\n<p><strong>Melanin-rich or shed feathers.</strong> Dark feathers carry inhibitors and shed feathers may lack follicular cells. Both raise the chance of a weak or absent result.</p>\n<p><strong>Mixed samples.</strong> Egg-membrane samples can carry maternal blood; cloacal swabs can carry environmental DNA. A mixed template may amplify but cannot be assigned cleanly.</p>\n<p><strong>Low-load pathogen samples.</strong> Intermittent shedding, a sampling site that missed the target, or a pathogen load below the limit of detection all produce weak signals. A negative there describes the sample, not the bird.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Does an inconclusive result mean the laboratory made a mistake?</strong>\nNot necessarily. It often means the sample could not support a reliable call. A laboratory that reports inconclusive is usually more honest than one that forces an answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I get a refund or a free retest?</strong>\nThat depends on the laboratory's policy and on whether the cause was sample quality or analytical failure. Ask before submitting.</p>\n<p><strong>Should I send another feather from the same bird?</strong>\nYes, if the first sample was shed, dirty or degraded. Use a freshly plucked feather with visible follicle tissue from a different tract.</p>\n<p><strong>Can an inconclusive test be repeated on the same extract?</strong>\nSometimes. If the cause is inhibition or a failed control, a re-extract or repeat can work. If the cause is too little DNA or a primer mismatch, the same extract will usually fail again.</p>\n<p><strong>Is an inconclusive result more common in certain species?</strong>\nYes. Ratites, species with closely sized CHD fragments and species with no validated primer set have higher rates. So do samples from shed feathers or degraded tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Does a negative result mean the bird is clean?</strong>\nIt means the target was not detected above the assay's limit of detection under a valid run. It does not prove absence, especially with low-load pathogens or a sampling site that missed the target.</p>\n<h2>The Author's Take</h2>\n<p><strong>Position.</strong> An inconclusive report is a quality signal, not a failure. A laboratory that never produces one is either working on unusually clean samples or overstating what its assay can support.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning.</strong> Low-template PCR is an exercise in uncertainty. The same extract can amplify on Monday and fail on Friday; a single tube can show a band that a replicate does not. The decision to withhold a call is the same decision that keeps a false result out of a studbook. Owners should read it that way, and laboratories should report the reason rather than hiding behind a word.</p>\n<p><strong>Disclosure.</strong> The views above are editorial. They are not a certification of any laboratory, a promise about any individual result, or veterinary advice; a veterinarian who knows the bird should make clinical decisions.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Bustin, S. A., Benes, V., Garson, J. A., Hellemans, J., Huggett, J., et al. (2009). The MIQE guidelines: minimum information for publication of quantitative real-time PCR experiments. <em>Clinical Chemistry</em>, 55(4), 611–622. https://doi.org/10.1373/clinchem.2008.112797</li>\n<li>Schrader, C., Schielke, A., Ellerbroek, L., &amp; Johne, R. (2012). PCR inhibitors: occurrence, properties and removal. <em>Journal of Applied Microbiology</em>, 113(5), 1014–1026. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2672.2012.05384.x</li>\n<li>Alaeddini, R. (2012). Forensic implications of PCR inhibition: a review. <em>Forensic Science International: Genetics</em>, 6(3), 297–305. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fsigen.2011.08.006</li>\n<li>Taberlet, P., Waits, L. P., &amp; Luikart, G. (1999). Noninvasive genetic sampling: look before you leap. <em>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</em>, 14(8), 323–327. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0169-5347(99)01637-7</li>\n<li>Rådström, P., Knutsson, R., Wolffs, P., Lövenklev, M., &amp; Löfström, C. (2004). Pre-PCR processing: strategies to generate PCR-compatible samples. <em>Molecular Biotechnology</em>, 26(2), 133–146. https://doi.org/10.1385/MB:26:2:133</li>\n<li>Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. (2012). <em>EP17-A2: Evaluation of Detection Capability for Clinical Laboratory Measurement Procedures</em>. https://clsi.org/standards/products/method-evaluation/documents/ep17/</li>\n<li>Turcu, M. C., Paștiu, A. I., Bel, L. V., &amp; Pusta, D. L. (2023). A comparison of feathers and oral swab samples as DNA sources for molecular sexing in companion birds. <em>Animals</em>, 13(3), 525. https://doi.org/10.3390/ani13030525</li>\n</ol>";
